        "geology": "Triassic shal and schist",
        "hydrology": "The drainage system of the Houay Pano catchment comprises a 1200 m perennial stream of irregular topography, which receives water from ephemeral waterways. The stream originates in the upper, mountainous part of the catchment and later becomes a tributary to the Houay Xon, which itself is a sub-tributary of the Num Dong before its confluence with the Mekong River near Luang Prabang.",
        "soils": "Soil distribution from hilltop to valley bottom comprises shallow Inceptisols along the crests, deep, clayey Ultisols (30%) and Alfisols (50%),and poorly drained clayey Dystrochrepts at the foot slopes and along the stream banks. Soil thickness increases from a few tens of centimers near the crests to few meters in the thalweg.",
        "vegetation": "Annual changes in land use were mostly related to shifting cultivation (with slash-and-burn method) from 1998 to 2015, and to the expansion of teak tree plantations since 2009. Overall, the areal percentages of annual crops and of forest respectively decreased from 26% to 4% and from 18% to 9%, while teak tree plantations increased from 2% to 34% from 1998 to 2019."
      },
